# Pass 100 §2 THE ANCHOR — `.@name` is not one of the three stances.
#
# This fixture is a REJECTION fixture and not a value fixture because the form
# it pins cannot be measured by a value: that is precisely how it shipped. On
# a native record `p.@x` answered `nil` where `p.x` answered `3`, and BOTH
# passed `duo check` — the parser stored the stance in the leading character
# of a field name, sema never looked, and codegen re-derived intent from that
# character and emitted a boxed metafield read against a value that has no
# metatable. A wrong value that type-checks leaves nothing for a corpus
# fixture to compare, so the pin has to be the diagnostic.
#
# §2 settles the anchor as one concept in three stances: bare `@` NAMES it,
# leading `.` WALKS from it, postfix `X@rel` MOVES it. `.@` is a fourth
# spelling of a concept that has exactly three, which A3 ONE EDGE forbids.

point: { x: i64, y: i64 }

main(): void
    p: point = { x = 3, y = 4 }
    print(p.@x)
end
